window.CASE = {"id":"harrowgate","stage1":{"briefing":"\n    <p class=\"dateline\">NORTH RIDING CONSTABULARY · C.I.D. · THURSDAY 1st JANUARY 1931 · 9.40 A.M.</p>\n    <h2>The death of Edmund Harrow</h2>\n    <img class=\"victim\" src=\"img/edmund.jpg\" alt=\"Edmund Harrow\">\n    <p>At half past midnight on New Year's morning, <b>Edmund Harrow</b>, 67, founder of Harrow &amp; Sons, clockmakers of York, was found dead on the floor of his private workshop at <b>Harrowgate Hall</b>, his house on the moors above the village of Thornby. The back of his skull had been broken by a single blow.</p>\n    <p>Snow closed the lane to Thornby at ten o'clock last night. Nobody has come or gone since, except Constable Barnes, who walked up from the village through the drifts at 1.40 a.m. after Julian Harrow telephoned the police house. The ground around the house was covered in unbroken fresh snow, and no stranger could have come near it without leaving a trail. <b>Whoever killed Edmund Harrow slept under his roof last night.</b></p>\n    <p>You are the first detectives up from York behind the snow plough. Barnes has kept the six members of the household apart and put together this file. He is a sound man. He's also a village constable, so check everything.</p>\n    <p>Note: <b>Dr Aldous Finch</b>, who examined the body, is one of the six. His medical notes are included. Weigh them with that in mind.</p>\n    <h3>How this case works</h3>\n    <p>The case comes in <b>three stages</b>, and each question is revealed only when you reach it. <b>Stage I:</b> Work out <em>when</em> Edmund Harrow was killed. The answer opens the rest of Barnes's inquiries.</p>\n    <p>Wrong answers aren't punished, but they're counted, and so are hints. Use the <b>Map</b>. Keep notes. Trust nobody's account of themselves.</p>\n    <p class=\"sig\">— Supt. R. Ackroyd, York</p>","suspects":[{"id":"lydia","name":"Lydia Harrow","age":36,"height":"5 ft 3 in","role":"The victim's daughter","bio":"Managing Director of Harrow &amp; Sons since 1927, when her father stepped back from the business. Runs the York office and holds the company seal. Unmarried, precise, keeps her feelings to herself. Has the <i>Financial Times</i> posted on to her wherever she is staying, and reads it over breakfast even on holiday.","interest":"Expected to inherit half the business. Her father had lately been \"looking over the books again\", according to her brother."},{"id":"julian","name":"Julian Harrow","age":32,"height":"6 ft 2 in","role":"The victim's son","bio":"Charming, idle, and in trouble with bookmakers in York and Doncaster. Has had no occupation since leaving Cambridge without a degree. His father has paid his debts three times.","interest":"Quarrelled loudly with his father after dinner. Heard to say: \"You'll be sorry you ever had a son.\""},{"id":"celeste","name":"Celeste Moreau","age":29,"height":"5 ft 6 in","role":"The victim's fiancée","bio":"Said to be Parisian. Until last spring a secretary at a London clock dealer's, where she met Edmund. Engaged to him in October; the wedding was set for March. Elegant, warm, and very attentive to her fiancé.","interest":"The household believes Edmund meant to change his will in her favour."},{"id":"pike","name":"Thomas Pike","age":51,"height":"5 ft 7 in","role":"Former apprentice, now a rival","bio":"Apprenticed to Edmund from 1895 to 1910, then left on bad terms. Now chief designer at the London office of the <b>Chronos Clock Corporation</b> of New York. He has always said the famous Harrow regulator escapement was his idea. This is the first time he has been invited back in twenty years, supposedly to make peace.","interest":"An old grudge, and a new employer that competes with Harrow &amp; Sons."},{"id":"finch","name":"Dr Aldous Finch","age":60,"height":"5 ft 10 in","role":"Family physician","bio":"The Harrows' doctor for twenty-five years and Edmund's regular chess partner. A widower. Kindly and exact. Lost most of his savings in the Crash of '29.","interest":"Edmund lent him £1,500 in 1929. Barnes can find no record that any of it was repaid."},{"id":"crane","name":"Mrs Margaret Crane","age":58,"height":"5 ft 2 in","role":"Housekeeper","bio":"Has kept Harrowgate Hall for twenty-two years. Fiercely loyal to \"the master\", suspicious of the fiancée, and not shy about saying so.","interest":"Miss Moreau wants to live in London. There has been talk of shutting up the Hall after the wedding and pensioning off the staff."}],"rooms":{"workshop":"<b>Old Coach House, now Edmund's workshop.</b> Across the courtyard from the house, with one door facing the house. Clocks cover the walls. The long-case <i>Harrow No. 1</i> regulator stands in the north-east corner. <em>Scene of the crime.</em>","courtyard":"<b>The courtyard.</b> Cobbled and walled on every side, about forty-five yards from the house to the workshop door. Covered in fresh snow last night. There are two doors into it from the house: the <b>Kitchen Door</b> and the <b>Boot Room Door</b>.","kitchen":"<b>Kitchen.</b> Its door opens onto the courtyard, and the coal bunker is just outside it. Another door leads east into the servants' passage. Mrs Crane and Dora made the midnight supper here.","passage":"<b>Servants' passage.</b> Runs along the back of the house from the kitchen to the boot room, with the cellar stair off it. The Music Room's north door opens onto it.","bootroom":"<b>Boot Room.</b> Coats, boots, galoshes, walking sticks and lanterns. One door to the servants' passage and one straight out into the courtyard.","billiard":"<b>Billiard Room.</b> Off the west side of the Great Hall.","library":"<b>Library.</b> Edmund's writing desk is here. The French window to the west lawn was bolted on the inside, and Barnes checked it. Directly above, on the first floor, is <b>Edmund's private study</b>, where his safe is.","hall":"<b>Great Hall.</b> Dinner was served here, and everyone was to gather here for the midnight unveiling of the <i>Harrow Perpetual</i>, which still stands under its velvet cover. The main staircase goes up to the first floor. The front door opens south onto the porch.","stairs":"<b>Back stairs.</b> Servants' stair to the first floor, reached from the passage.","music":"<b>Music Room.</b> Houses the late Mrs Eleanor Harrow's Steinway <b>Duo-Art reproducing piano</b>, which can be played by hand or <i>will play itself</i> from perforated paper rolls, and a cabinet of those rolls. Double doors lead south into the Drawing Room, and a plain door north into the servants' passage.","drawing":"<b>Drawing Room.</b> A tall south window looks straight down the garden and the yew walk to the gazebo. Double doors lead north to the Music Room and east to the Conservatory.","conservatory":"<b>Conservatory.</b> A glass annex off the Drawing Room, with a door out to the garden.","porch":"<b>Front porch.</b> Faces south onto the carriage drive.","garden":"<b>Garden.</b> Lawns and a yew walk run down to a stone gazebo about sixty yards from the house. The house and the courtyard wall lie between the garden and the courtyard, so <b>the workshop cannot be seen from the garden</b>.","gazebo":"<b>Gazebo.</b> A stone summer-house at the end of the yew walk. From it you look back at the whole south front of the house."},"evidence":[{"id":"e-barnes","kind":"report","title":"Constable Barnes: summary of the evening","sub":"The account the whole household agrees on","html":"\n      <p class=\"typed\">Compiled 1st Jan 1931, 6 a.m., by P.C. 212 W. Barnes, Thornby.</p>\n      <table class=\"tl\">\n        <tr><td>6.10 p.m.</td><td>A telegram for Mr Harrow is brought up by the post-lad on his bicycle before the lane closes. Mrs Crane takes it in and hands it to Mr Harrow in the Library. It has not been found.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>8.00</td><td>Dinner in the Great Hall, all seven present. Several say Mr Harrow was \"unusually quiet\".</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>9.30</td><td>Mr Harrow and Mr Julian Harrow quarrel in the Library. Raised voices are heard in the Hall.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>10.00</td><td>Snow very heavy. The lane to Thornby is impassable from about this time.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>10.15</td><td>Coffee in the Drawing Room for everyone except Mr Harrow, who stays in the Library writing letters until about 10.55.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>c. 11.00</td><td>The party breaks up. Mr Harrow says he has final adjustments to make in the workshop, will be back for midnight, and is <b>not to be disturbed</b>.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>11.45</td><td>Everyone gathers in the Great Hall for the unveiling, as planned, except Mr Julian Harrow, who has been sent to the cellar.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>11.50</td><td>Miss Lydia Harrow gives the toast.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>12.00</td><td>Midnight. Mr Harrow does not appear.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>12.25 a.m.</td><td>Mr Julian Harrow and Dr Finch go out to the workshop to fetch him.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>12.30</td><td>They find him dead. Dr Finch examines him at 12.40.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>12.45</td><td>Mr Julian Harrow telephones Thornby police house.</td></tr>\n      </table>\n      <p class=\"typed\">I arrived at 1.40 a.m. by the front drive and roped off the courtyard at once. Nobody has walked on it since. I am making a full survey of the tracks in the snow and will send it on.</p>"},{"id":"e-medical","kind":"report","title":"Dr Finch's medical notes","sub":"Written at 12.40 a.m., beside the body","html":"\n      <div class=\"hand\">\n      <p>E. H.: examined 12.40 a.m. in the workshop, by paraffin lamp, which I relit.</p>\n      <p><b>Cause:</b> one heavy blow to the back of the skull, left side, with something blunt and weighty. Death must have been almost instant. No defensive injuries. He did not see it coming, or did not believe it was coming.</p>\n      <p><b>Condition:</b> the workshop was bitterly cold. The stove was almost out and the lamp had been put out. Hands and face cold; body still warm under the clothes. Rigor only just perceptible in the jaw.</p>\n      <p><b>Blood:</b> a pool under the head, well congealed, with a skin over it and <u>dry at the edges</u>. It is not fresh.</p>\n      <p><b>Time:</b> in a cold room like this I would put death at one to two hours before my examination, so roughly <b>10.40 to 11.40</b>. I would not swear to within half an hour either way. I <i>will</i> swear he had not died ten minutes before we found him.</p>\n      <p class=\"sig\">A. Finch, M.D.</p>\n      </div>"},{"id":"e-scene","kind":"report","title":"Workshop: inventory of the scene","sub":"Constable Barnes, with sketch","html":"\n      \n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 280\" class=\"sketch\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Constable's sketch of the workshop floor\">\n  <rect x=\"10\" y=\"10\" width=\"400\" height=\"250\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n  <line x1=\"185\" y1=\"260\" x2=\"235\" y2=\"260\" stroke=\"var(--paper)\" stroke-width=\"5\"/>\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"276\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"sk-s\">DOOR (faces house)</text>\n  <rect x=\"14\" y=\"60\" width=\"40\" height=\"170\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"sk-s\" transform=\"rotate(-90 34 150)\">WORKBENCH</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"16\" width=\"36\" height=\"30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"sk-n\">10</text>\n  <text x=\"38\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"sk-s\">stove</text>\n  <circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"92\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"sk-n\">9</text>\n  <rect x=\"352\" y=\"16\" width=\"44\" height=\"26\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"374\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"sk-n\">7</text>\n  <text x=\"374\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"sk-s\">regulator</text>\n  <g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\">\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"22\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"22\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"22\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"22\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"22\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"22\" r=\"6\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"402\" cy=\"90\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"402\" cy=\"130\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"402\" cy=\"170\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"402\" cy=\"210\" r=\"6\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"42\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"sk-s\">wall clocks (8)</text>\n  <ellipse cx=\"338\" cy=\"70\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"var(--blood)\" opacity=\".55\"/>\n  <text x=\"312\" y=\"92\" class=\"sk-n\">3</text>\n  <path d=\"M338 62 l-10 10 -60 50 -30 40 m30 -40 l-30 -10 m36 3 l20 40 m-100 7 l-14 20 m14 -20 l18 22\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 4\"/>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"150\" class=\"sk-n\">1</text>\n  <rect x=\"200\" y=\"88\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"194\" y=\"84\" class=\"sk-n\">4</text>\n  <rect x=\"352\" y=\"84\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"370\" y=\"104\" class=\"sk-n\">5</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"252\" class=\"sk-n\">11</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"248\" r=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"248\" class=\"sk-s\">N ↑</text>\n</svg>\n      <ol class=\"inv\">\n        <li><b>The body</b> of Edmund Harrow, face down, head towards the north-east corner, at the foot of the regulator (7).</li>\n        <li><b>Wound:</b> back of the head, left side. A single blow.</li>\n        <li><b>Blood:</b> a pool about fourteen inches across under the head, congealed.</li>\n        <li><b>Brass clock-weight</b>, 9 lb, normally kept on the bench, lying on the floor three feet from the body. Wiped clean, except for blood in the hook-eye. Almost certainly the weapon.</li>\n        <li><b>French carriage clock</b>, brass with glass panels, normally kept on the workbench. Found on the floor at the edge of the blood pool. All the glass is smashed and the movement is broken. <b>Hands stopped at 11.52.</b> The broken glass lies <u>on top of</u> the blood, and there is no blood under or on the underside of any piece.</li>\n        <li><b>Mr Harrow's pocket-watch</b>, a gold half-hunter, in his waistcoat pocket. Still running and correct.</li>\n        <li><b>The long-case regulator</b> (<i>\"Harrow No. 1\", 1899</i>) in the north-east corner. <b>Pendulum still. Hands at 11.21.</b> The glass of the trunk door is cracked, and there is a smear of blood with grey hairs on the door's edge about five feet from the floor. Mrs Crane says: \"That clock has not stopped since the old Queen died. The master wound it every Sunday of his life.\"</li>\n        <li><b>Wall clocks</b>, eight of them, all running and all correct to within a minute when checked at 12.40 a.m. Mr Harrow kept every clock in the workshop set to the regulator.</li>\n        <li><b>Paraffin lamp</b> on the bench. It had been <u>put out</u>, not burnt dry, as there is plenty of oil left. The chimney glass was cold at 12.40.</li>\n        <li><b>Iron stove.</b> Almost out, a few embers left. There is burnt paper in the firebox. Set aside for examination.</li>\n        <li><b>Mr Harrow's lantern</b>, just inside the door. Out.</li>\n        <li><b>Door</b>: unlocked, no key. The only way in or out.</li>\n      </ol>"},{"id":"e-programme","kind":"document","title":"The evening's programme card","sub":"Printed card, one at every place at dinner","html":"\n      <div class=\"card-print\">\n        <p class=\"cp-top\">HARROWGATE HALL</p>\n        <p class=\"cp-sub\">New Year's Eve · MCMXXX</p>\n        <hr>\n        <p>Eight o'clock: <b>Dinner</b></p>\n        <p>A quarter to twelve: <b>The company assembles in the Great Hall</b></p>\n        <p>Ten minutes to twelve: <b>A Toast</b>, proposed by Miss Lydia Harrow</p>\n        <p>Midnight: <b>The Harrow Perpetual unveiled</b><br><i>A clock that will outlast us all</i></p>\n        <hr>\n        <p class=\"cp-sub\">Supper to follow</p>\n      </div>"},{"id":"w-dora","kind":"statement","who":"Dora Pell, kitchen maid (16)","title":"Statement: Dora Pell","sub":"Kitchen maid, a witness only","html":"\n      <p>\"I went out for coal just as the master went out, and that'd be about eight minutes past eleven. The snow had stopped and the yard was all smooth, like a new tablecloth. Not a mark on it but his. He went slow with his stick and his lantern, and I saw the workshop windows go yellow when he lit the lamp inside.</p>\n      <p>Then I came back in and did pastry with Mrs Crane till the quarter to twelve. I never left her and she never left me, sir, I'll swear to it on anything.\"</p>"},{"id":"s1-lydia","kind":"statement","who":"lydia","title":"Statement: Lydia Harrow","sub":"Taken 2.30 a.m.","html":"\n      <p>\"Father was tired at dinner. He'd had some business telegram, I assume. He didn't say what. And Julian upset him, as Julian always does.</p>\n      <p>After coffee I went through to the Music Room, at about five past eleven, and played. Mostly Mother's Chopin. I asked Dr Finch to keep the doors shut. I'm badly out of practice and I can't bear an audience. At about twenty to twelve I stopped and went to fetch everybody for the unveiling. I gave the toast at ten to. When Father hadn't come at midnight I assumed he was still fussing over the Perpetual. At twenty past I sent Julian and Dr Finch to fetch him.</p>\n      <p>Julian told me the clock on the floor had stopped at eight minutes to twelve. Julian was in the cellar then. I'm not suggesting anything, Constable. I'm only telling you where he was.\"</p>"},{"id":"s1-julian","kind":"statement","who":"julian","title":"Statement: Julian Harrow","sub":"Taken 2.45 a.m.","html":"\n      <p>\"Yes, we rowed, and everyone in the house will tell you so. I asked for a loan. He said 'not one penny more' and I said a few things I'd rather have back.</p>\n      <p>From eleven I was in the Billiard Room on my own, knocking the balls about. At about a quarter to twelve Mrs Crane collared me because we were a bottle short of the '21 Pol Roger for the toast, so down to the cellar I went. It took me a good ten minutes to find in that tomb. I came up after Lydia's speech, which annoyed her.</p>\n      <p>At twenty-five past twelve Lydia sent Finch and me to fetch the old man. We went out through the kitchen and across the yard, and found him. I saw the carriage clock on the floor, eight minutes to twelve, and I felt sick, because I knew exactly how that would look for me.</p>\n      <p>Lydia playing Chopin for half an hour? That's news to me. She gave up lessons when she was twelve and swore she'd never touch a piano again. Well, good for her.\"</p>"},{"id":"s1-celeste","kind":"statement","who":"celeste","title":"Statement: Celeste Moreau","sub":"Taken 3.00 a.m.","html":"\n      <p>\"I had a headache from the champagne. At eleven I went up to my room and lay in the dark until about a quarter to twelve, when I heard Lydia calling everybody down to the Hall.</p>\n      <p>I loved Edmund. We were to be married in March. Why would I hurt the only thing I had in the world?\"</p>"},{"id":"s1-pike","kind":"statement","who":"pike","title":"Statement: Thomas Pike","sub":"Taken 3.10 a.m.","html":"\n      <p>\"From about five past eleven I sat in the Conservatory with a book. I'm not a party man. I could hear the piano. At a quarter to twelve I went through to the Hall with the rest.</p>\n      <p>Yes, I believe the No. 1 escapement was my design. I said so in 1910 and I'd say it today. But that was twenty years ago, Constable. I'd hardly come all this way to kill a man over it on New Year's Eve.\"</p>"},{"id":"s1-finch","kind":"statement","who":"finch","title":"Statement: Dr Aldous Finch","sub":"Taken 3.20 a.m.","html":"\n      <p>\"I stepped out onto the front porch to smoke at about five past eleven. The snow had stopped only minutes before. I had swept the steps at half past ten and there was a fresh half-inch on them, clean as a sheet. I was back in by ten past and sat down in the Drawing Room with a book.</p>\n      <p>Lydia was already playing in the next room. She'd asked me to keep the double doors shut. I must say she played beautifully, and I'd no idea she could. Not a wrong note for twenty minutes or more. At about half past there was a pause, and I supposed she was choosing more music. When she began again she was a good deal less sure of herself. Champagne, I dare say. She came through at about twenty to twelve and we went to the Hall together.</p>\n      <p>At twenty-five past twelve Julian and I went out to the workshop. My notes on the body are with the Constable.</p>\n      <p>The loan? Yes, Edmund lent me money. He never once asked for it back, and he never would have.\"</p>"},{"id":"s1-crane","kind":"statement","who":"crane","title":"Statement: Mrs Margaret Crane","sub":"Taken 3.40 a.m.","html":"\n      <p>\"I took that telegram in at ten past six and gave it into the master's own hand in the Library. He read it and went white as a sheet, and he didn't come out again until dinner.</p>\n      <p>At about ten past eleven he came through my kitchen with his lantern and said he was going to the workshop and wasn't to be disturbed before midnight. Dora and I were at the pastry for the midnight supper from eleven until after the quarter to twelve. Neither of us left that kitchen, except Dora for the coal just as the master went out.</p>\n      <p>At a quarter to twelve I found we were a bottle short for the toast and sent Mr Julian down to the cellar.</p>\n      <p>As for <i>her</i>\" <span class=\"aside\">(meaning Miss Moreau)</span> \"she'll have the Hall shut up and the lot of us out on the moor by Easter. I'm not ashamed to say it. But I'd not have laid a finger on him, not for the world.\"</p>"}],"question":{"kind":"time","prompt":"At what time was Edmund Harrow killed?","help":"Give the time to the minute (e.g. 10:47).","hints":["Two clocks in the workshop disagree about the time. Only one of them was standing where he fell.","In the scene inventory, look at what is lying on top of what."]}},"locked":{"s2":"","s3":"","sol":""}};
